# `Orderable`

`Orderable` is an **abstract type with laws**, narrowed by ordinary `<:` — not a
bound, a capability or a trait. Its law is that any two values of the type can be
put in order. A type narrows it when its values carry an ordering key of their
own:

```hql
Int <: Orderable
Float <: Orderable
String <: Orderable
Doc <: Orderable
Card <: Orderable
Hit <: Orderable
```

Collections and [[data-type|Data]] do not. The distinction is the one
[[type-system]] draws between ancestry and capability: the key is a property of
the value, not something registered for it from outside.

`Orderable` declares no field and prescribes no representation: it is the one
member of `Type` in `src/types.rs` with no corresponding `Value`. The law is
enforced there behaviorally, by `order_key` in `src/values.rs`, rather than
checked from a declaration — see [[type-system]], *Where the laws live*.

## Ordered is not orderable

`Orderable` is a property of a **value**, not of a collection. A `Seq<T>` is
*ordered* — a position exists for every element — whether or not `T` is
`Orderable` at all: `Seq<Function>` has a first and a second element and no two
functions have a meaningful `<`. The two are not the same claim and neither
implies the other. See [[hql/collections]], *Ordered is not orderable*, for the
three-way distinction and for `sort` as a signature rather than a supertype.

## Why a card is orderable

A card carries its own key: its **name**. That is what makes a prefix of the
vault reproducible without vault order becoming observable.

```hql
cards | take(5)
```

`cards : Set<Card>` stands — see [[hql/collections]] — *and* `take(5)` over it is
well defined, because the order comes from the values rather than from how the
files were found. Discovering documents still establishes no ordering guarantee.

The question was never `Set` versus `Seq`. It was whether a reproducible prefix
requires an observable vault order, and it does not.

## `take`

- Over a `Set`, `take(n)` imposes the canonical order the elements' keys give.
- Over a `Seq`, `take(n)` **keeps the order the sequence already has.**
  Re-imposing the canonical order there would silently discard the `sort` that
  produced it.
- Over elements that do not narrow `Orderable`, `take` is a type error that says
  to sort with an explicit key first.

## Ties

A `Hit` orders by score descending, then by its card's key, so equal scores do
not make a prefix depend on load order. `Score` is a `Float` and ties are
ordinary at realistic precision, so a ranked collection must state its tiebreak
rather than inherit one — see [[hql/semantic-search]].

## Open

- Whether `Orderable` is a single total order per type or a type may offer
  several named keys. Sorting expects a **total** order; a partial one leaves the
  result undetermined, so this question is not cosmetic.
- Card key collisions across namespaces, and whether the key is the name or the
  qualified path.
- Whether user-declared types may narrow `Orderable`, and how they state the key.

Implemented in `src/types.rs` (`is_orderable`), `src/values.rs` (`order_key`)
and `src/evaluation.rs`. Tests in `tests/vault.rs`.
